parsed records up to violation, i.e., the valid prefix, and then the violation line. But also the \"valid_prefix\" is mentioned. So I'll return: valid (bool), records (list of parsed dicts for all lines that were successfully parsed before violation or entire if valid), violation_line (int or None), valid_prefix (list of records before violation). Actually, \"valid_prefix\" is essentially the records up to but not including the violation line. So we can just include that.\n\nBut to simplify and avoid overcomplicating, I'll follow a typical pattern: return a dict with \"valid\": bool, \"trajectory\": list of dicts (all parsed lines, with a \"valid\" field per record? No, that's too much). Instead, I'll output \"valid\": bool, \"records\": list of dicts (all parsed lines, even invalid ones? But the validation stops at first violation, so we only parse until that point. So records will be the valid prefix. Then we also output \"violation_line\": the line number where violation occurred (None if valid). That matches the description: \"Return lowercase typed trajectory records and an explicit validity flag. On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" So we return the valid prefix as \"records\", and the line number as \"violation_line\". If valid, \"records\" is the full list, \"violation_line\" is None.\n\nThus, the implementation: iterate lines, for each line, attempt to match with regex or split on first colon. Determine type and content. Check expected type order. If matches expected, add to records. If not, set violation_line to current index (1-based) and stop. Return records list (valid prefix) and violation_line. Return \"valid\": violation_line is None.\n\nWe also need to ensure that after Final, no more lines. So if we encounter Final, we should check that it's the last line? The order validation should handle that: after Final, we set expected to None and any further line is violation. So after Final, any next line is violation.\n\nAlso, we must ensure that the last record is Final? Actually, the trajectory can be a sequence of cycles ending with Final. The description: \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" So if there is no Final, the trajectory is incomplete? The problem doesn't specify that Final is required. Possibly a valid trajectory could be just cycles without Final? Probably it expects Final at the end. But the fixtures all end with \"Final: done\". So we'll require that the last line is Final. If not, that's a violation at the end. However, the order validation will allow a cycle to end with Observation, and then if there is no Final, the sequence ends. Should that be invalid? The problem says \"Permit Final only at the end\". It doesn't say it must be present. But typical ReAct includes Final. Given the fixture, all have Final. So we'll enforce that the last line must be Final, otherwise violation at the end (line after last? Actually, missing Final would be a violation at line N+1? But we can just check after parsing all lines if the last record is not Final, then it's a violation? The description \"On the first violation\" - if the trajectory ends without Final, the violation would be at the line after last? That's tricky. Alternatively, we can treat the absence of Final as a violation at the end. But the problem says \"first violation\", so if the trajectory is valid up to last line but no Final, the violation is after all lines? That doesn't line up. I think it's safer to require that the sequence ends with Final. If not, we can consider it invalid at the point of the last line (i.e., after processing all lines, if the last record is not Final, then it's a violation at line N? But that line itself is not a violation; the missing Final is not a line. So to follow \"first violation\", we can treat the end of input as a violation if the expected state is something else. But we can simply require that the last record must be Final. If not, then after parsing all lines, we have a violation because the sequence is incomplete. We could report the violation as the line after the last line (N+1). But that might not be a line. I think we'll just enforce that after processing all lines, if the last record is not Final, then we set violation_line to len(lines)+1? That seems arbitrary. Instead, we can just not require Final; the problem says \"Permit Final only at the end\", meaning it's allowed but not required? Actually, \"Permit Final only at the end\" means Final is only allowed at the end, but it doesn't say it must be there. However, the fixture has Final. I think it's safer to require Final at end because the description says \"stop grammar\". We'll design such that if the sequence ends without Final, it's invalid. But how to report the violation line? We can treat the end as a violation at line N+1, but then there is no valid prefix beyond N lines. I think it's more consistent to parse the entire list, then check if the last record is Final. If not, then the violation occurs at the last line (the one that is not Final) because that line should have been Final. Actually, suppose the last line is an Observation. Then that Observation is valid in itself, but the sequence should have been followed by a Thought or Final. So the violation is that after the Observation, no further line is present. So the violation is at the end, not at the last line. To simplify, we can just check after parsing all lines that the expected state is \"after Final\" (i.e., no expected next type). If the expected state after processing all lines is not None (i.e., we expect something else), then it's incomplete, and we can set violation_line to len(lines) + 1? But the problem says line numbers, so maybe we set it to len(lines) (the last line) because the lack of continuation is a violation on that line? That seems odd. Another approach: Reject missing Final only if the sequence has some cycles but not terminated. I think the simplest is to follow the fixture: all cases have Final. So we can assume Final is present and at the end. If not, raise ValueError? But the instruction says raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case. So if a case doesn't have Final, we can raise ValueError. However, the objective says \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" That suggests returning, not raising. I'm leaning towards returning an object with valid=False and violation info. So for missing Final, we can set violation_line to the length of lines + 1 (or the last line) and valid_prefix as all records. But let's decide: We'll parse and validate order. After parsing all lines, if the expected state is not \"None\" (i.e., we are still expecting something), then we have a violation at the end. We'll set violation_line = len(lines) + 1 (or 0? The spec says one-based line, so maybe we use length+1 to indicate beyond last line). But that might not be a valid line number. Alternatively, we can treat it as violation at the last line because the trajectory ended prematurely. I'll go with setting violation_line to len(lines) (the last line) and valid_prefix is all records except the last? No, because the last line is valid in isolation. This is messy.\n\nGiven the time, I'll implement a straightforward approach: parse all lines, validate order, if any violation, record violation line and stop. After processing all lines, if the last record is not Final, then set violation_line to len(lines)+1 and valid_prefix to all records? But then the valid_prefix includes the last record which is not Final? It's valid up to that point, but the violation is that the trajectory is incomplete. I think it's acceptable to report violation at the last line if no Final, because the last line is not at the end of a complete sequence. I'll treat the expected state after each line. If after processing a line, the next expected is something, and there is no next line, then the violation is at the line where the next was expected, i.e., the last line? Actually, the last line is the one that brought the state to expecting something else. So the violation is that the last line did not complete the cycle properly. So we can set violation_line = last_line_number and valid_prefix = records before that last line? But then the last line is excluded from valid prefix. That is fine because the last line is the one causing the violation? In the case of a line that is out of order, the violation line is that line and valid prefix is before it. So for missing Final, the last line is, say, an Observation. Then we can consider that the violation occurs at that Observation because it should have been followed by something else? But the Observation itself is valid, but the sequence is incomplete. So maybe the violation is at the line after the Observation, which doesn't exist. So better to treat the end as a violation at line (len(lines)+1). I'll implement that: after the loop, if expected_state is not None (meaning we still expect a record), then set violation_line = len(lines) + 1 and valid_prefix = records (all records). Then valid=False.\n\nBut the problem says \"first violation\", so if the last line is out of order, it would be caught during parsing. If all lines pass order but the last is not Final, then the first violation is after the last line, which is line(len+1). I'll go with that.\n\nAlso, lines that don't match any pattern: we treat as violation at that line.\n\nWe'll use a function parse_line that returns (type, content) or raises ValueError. We'll skip lines that are empty? Fixture lines are non-empty.\n\nNow, coding the solve function. We'll return a dict with \"valid\" bool, \"records\" list, \"violation_line\" int or None, \"valid_prefix\" list (same as records if invalid, else None? Actually, if valid, there is no violation, so we can omit violation_line and valid_prefix, or set to None). I'll just include all keys with None when not applicable.\n\nNow, structure the JSON output: The entire response must be exactly one valid JSON object with single key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing Python source code. So we need to produce a JSON string that contains the Python code, with proper escaping of newlines, backslashes, quotes. For convenience, we can write the Python code as a multi-line string and then in the JSON, escape it. Since we are writing the response, we need to ensure that the Python code itself is correctly escaped as JSON string. We'll do it manually.\n\nI'll write the Python code, then embed it in a JSON object. But note: The response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\". So the final output should be something like {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\\n\"}. I need to escape newlines as \\n and backslashes as \\\\, and quotes as \\\".\n\nI'll write the code as a plain string in my mind, then escape it.\n\nFirst, write the Python code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case[\"lines\"]\n    records = []\n    expected = \"thought\"  # start expecting Thought\n    violation_line = None\n    valid_prefix = []\n    line_number = 0\n    for line in lines:\n        line_number += 1\n        # parse line\n        if line.startswith(\"Thought:\"):\n            typed = \"thought\"\n            content = line[len(\"Thought:\"):].strip()\n        elif line.startswith(\"Action:\"):\n            typed = \"action\"\n            content = line[len(\"Action:\"):].strip()\n        elif line.startswith(\"Observation:\"):\n            typed = \"observation\"\n            content = line[len(\"Observation:\"):].strip()\n        elif line.startswith(\"Final:\"):\n            typed = \"final\"\n            content = line[len(\"Final:\"):].strip()\n        else:\n            # unrecognized line\n            violation_line = line_number\n            valid_prefix = records[:]\n            break\n        # check expected\n        if typed != expected:\n            violation_line = line_number\n            valid_prefix = records[:]\n            break\n        # add record\n        records.append({\"type\": typed, \"content\": content})\n        # update expected state\n        if typed == \"thought\":\n            expected = \"action\"\n        elif typed == \"action\":\n            expected = \"observation\"\n        elif typed == \"observation\":\n            expected = \"thought\"  # next cycle or final\n        elif typed == \"final\":\n            expected = None  # end\n        # if expected is None, then after final, any further line would be violation (caught by next iteration)\n    else:\n        # loop finished without break\n        if expected is not None:\n            # incomplete trajectory - missing final or additional records\n            violation_line = line_number + 1  # line after last\n            valid_prefix = records[:]\n        else:\n            # fully valid\n            pass\n    # Build result\n    result = {\n        \"valid\": violation_line is None,\n        \"records\": records if violation_line is None else valid_prefix,\n        \"violation_line\": violation_line,\n        \"valid_prefix\": valid_prefix if violation_line is not None else None\n    }\n    return result\n```\n\nBut note: In the else clause, we used line_number which is the last line. If the loop ended without breaking and expected is None, then valid. If expected is not None, we set violation_line to line_number+1. That's fine.\n\nAlso, we must not mutate case. We only read case[\"lines\"] and case might have other keys, but we don't modify.\n\nWe need to ensure we import nothing extra; only standard library.\n\nPotential edge: If lines is empty? Not in fixture. But handle: if no lines, expected is \"thought\" initially, so after loop, expected not None, violation_line=1, valid_prefix=[].\n\nNow, also need to handle that after Final, expected is None, so if there are more lines, the next line will be checked against expected=None, but our condition `if typed != expected` will trigger because typed will be something, expected is None. That will cause violation at that line, which is correct.\n\nNow, the result should be JSON-serializable. The dict with keys: valid (bool), records (list of dicts), violation_line (int or None), valid_prefix (list or None). That's fine.\n\nBut is there any other requirement? The objective says \"Return lowercase typed trajectory records and an explicit validity flag.\" That's exactly what we have. Also \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" So we return those.\n\nNow, we need to produce the JSON string. I'll write the Python code as a string with triple quotes? But in JSON, we need to escape. I'll write the code as a single line with \\n escapes.\n\nI'll use a raw string in my mind and then escape.\n\nTo avoid errors, I'll construct the JSON manually.\n\nThe final output should be:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lines = case[\\\"lines\\\"]\\n    records = []\\n    expected = \\\"thought\\\"\\n    violation_line = None\\n    valid_prefix = []\\n    line_number = 0\\n    for line in lines:\\n        line_number += 1\\n        if line.startswith(\\\"Thought:\\\"):\\n            typed = \\\"thought\\\"\\n            content = line[len(\\\"Thought:\\\"):].strip()\\n        elif line.startswith(\\\"Action:\\\"):\\n            typed = \\\"action\\\"\\n            content = line[len(\\\"Action:\\\"):].strip()\\n        elif line.startswith(\\\"Observation:\\\"):\\n            typed = \\\"observation\\\"\\n            content = line[len(\\\"Observation:\\\"):].strip()\\n        elif line.startswith(\\\"Final:\\\"):\\n            typed = \\\"final\\\"\\n            content = line[len(\\\"Final:\\\"):].strip()\\n        else:\\n            violation_line = line_number\\n            valid_prefix = records[:]\\n            break\\n        if typed != expected:\\n            violation_line = line_number\\n            valid_prefix = records[:]\\n            break\\n        records.append({\\\"type\\\": typed, \\\"content\\\": content})\\n        if typed == \\\"thought\\\":\\n            expected = \\\"action\\\"\\n        elif typed == \\\"action\\\":\\n            expected = \\\"observation\\\"\\n        elif typed == \\\"observation\\\":\\n            expected = \\\"thought\\\"\\n        elif typed == \\\"final\\\":\\n            expected = None\\n    else:\\n        if expected is not None:\\n            violation_line = line_number + 1\\n            valid_prefix = records[:]\\n    result = {\\n        \\\"valid\\\": violation_line is None,\\n        \\\"records\\\": records if violation_line is None else valid_prefix,\\n        \\\"violation_line\\\": violation_line,\\n        \\\"valid_prefix\\\": valid_prefix if violation_line is not None else None\\n    }\\n    return result\\n\"}\n\nBut we need to ensure that quotes inside the string are escaped: double quotes become \\\". Also backslashes: there are none except the escaped ones. The string contains double quotes within, so we need to escape them. Also the line breaks are represented as \\n.\n\nWe can also use a raw string in Python but not in JSON. So we do it manually.\n\nCheck for any unescaped double quotes: In the code, we have e.g., case[\"lines\"] -> that should be case[\\\"lines\\\"] in the JSON string. Similarly, \"type\": typed -> \\\"type\\\": typed. Also the dictionary keys in the result have quotes.\n\nAlso, note that we have \"thought\" etc., those are quoted.\n\nTo avoid errors, I'll write the Python code using single quotes where possible, but the problem doesn't restrict that. However, using single quotes inside a double-quoted JSON string is fine because double quotes are the only ones that need escaping. We can write Python code with single quotes for strings to reduce escaping. For example, we can use 'thought' instead of \"thought\". But careful: the line prefixes are with double quotes in the fixture? Actually, the lin